    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "There are 50 envelopes, each containing one piece of paper with either 1 or $-1$ written on it. John cannot see the number in each individual envelope, but he can pick any 3 envelopes and then an oracle will tell him the product of the 3 numbers hidden in these envelopes. What is the least number of times John has to repeat the procedure in order to find out the product of all 50 numbers hidden in the envelopes?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "17"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "18"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "19"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "25"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "48"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Parity argument",
        "body": "**18 questions suffice.** Ask for envelopes $\\{1,2,3\\}, \\{1,2,4\\}, \\{1,2,5\\}$ and then split envelopes 6 to 50 (45 envelopes) into 15 groups of three. Multiply all 18 answers: envelopes 1 and 2 appear three times each, all others once. Since $x^2 = 1$ for $x = \\pm1$, the product of the answers equals $x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_{50}$.\n\n**Fewer do not.** The total product can only be found if some of the asked triples multiply to it, i.e. if we can choose some of the triples so that every envelope appears an **odd** number of times in them. (Otherwise one can change some of the numbers so that all answers stay the same but the total product changes.) If $T$ triples are chosen, the 50 odd counts add up to $3T$; a sum of 50 odd numbers is even, so $T$ is even. Also every envelope appears at least once, so $3T \\ge 50$, $T \\ge 17$. The smallest even such $T$ is 18.\n\n**Answer: (B) 18**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "logic",
      "number-theory"
    ],
    "difficulty": 3,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": "The official Canadian answer key gives (A) 17, but 17 questions cannot suffice: the chosen triples must cover every envelope an odd number of times, so the number of triples is even and at least 50/3; 18 is achievable. Stored answer B (18); please check."
  },
